1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,955
At home they had learned to write down who did what; the office preferred a deadline to a promise.

2
00:00:05,955 --> 00:00:14,274
The fourth arc began downtown, in a spacious office with a gigantic carpet and a view of the orbital tower.

3
00:00:14,274 --> 00:00:22,318
A prominent businessman said “hi” to them without looking up, and the management team arrived ten minutes later.

4
00:00:22,318 --> 00:00:28,362
“We want a film about our new technological platform,” the woman said.

5
00:00:28,362 --> 00:00:42,025
“You will supervise the script, and you will receive a small fee.” Various people around the table produced a phone and took notes, like a boy at his first interview.

6
00:00:42,025 --> 00:00:45,285
The agenda was mostly financial.

7
00:00:45,285 --> 00:00:56,013
A secretary took the minutes; a broker consulted his phone; a reactionary older man wanted to commercialize everything before the test.

8
00:00:56,013 --> 00:00:59,908
“Progress is not a pace,” Lin said.

9
00:00:59,908 --> 00:01:16,811
“It is a direction.” “Then straighten your stride,” the businessman replied, “and delete the last paragraph.” From that morning the work would be supervised by the client, not by the university.

10
00:01:16,811 --> 00:01:26,464
Their departure from the campus was compulsory for the summer: an internship, they were told, would teach them more than a lecture.

11
00:01:26,464 --> 00:01:34,538
A young daughter of the union organizer worked there too, and a boy from the mailroom applied for the same post.

12
00:01:34,538 --> 00:01:40,787
“You will discover,” said the woman, “that most of what we make is invisible.

13
00:01:40,787 --> 00:01:44,847
That is what makes it hard to manage.”

14
00:01:44,847 --> 00:01:56,055
The work itself was to administrate a small civic tool: a tool that would industrialize nothing, defy the market, and never be profitable.

15
00:01:56,055 --> 00:01:59,480
“It will fail,” the broker said.

16
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:04,224
“It does not need to be profitable,” Maya replied.

17
00:02:04,224 --> 00:02:12,843
“It needs to be accurate.” Without that, the whole platform was a film about a building.

18
00:02:12,843 --> 00:02:17,948
Wealth descended and rose around them, and the young persisted.

19
00:02:17,948 --> 00:02:22,287
“We do not receive praise here,” she said.

20
00:02:22,287 --> 00:02:35,000
“We receive deadlines.” A farewell lunch was held for the last intern, and an email went upwards and downwards through the company before he had finished his coffee.

21
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:46,159
A big question imposed itself: could a pupil from a public school succeed here, where a secretarial salary was a dime above the housework rate?

22
00:02:46,159 --> 00:02:52,108
Nobody could say what would happen next, and nobody wanted to lead with that.

23
00:02:52,108 --> 00:03:02,705
His face, like hers, said that the work would continue without them, and that this was the price of a real deadline.
